# Model Details

The project compares classical econometrics with regularized and nonlinear machine learning.

## ARIMA

ARIMA provides the classical time-series benchmark.

It captures:

```text
Autoregressive Behaviour
        +
Differencing
        +
Moving Average Dynamics
```

A grid of `(p,d,q)` combinations is evaluated and the specification is selected using training-set AIC.

## Ridge Regression

Ridge applies L2 regularization:

```text
Prediction Error
+
α × Coefficient Magnitude²
```

The project evaluates:

```text
α = 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 500, 1000, 5000
```

Strong regularization is particularly relevant because lagged macroeconomic variables can be highly correlated.

## Random Forest

Random Forest is used to capture nonlinear relationships while controlling variance.

The model is deliberately constrained using shallow trees and minimum leaf-size restrictions.

## Decision Tree

A shallow decision tree is used as an interpretability benchmark.

```text
max_depth = 1
```

This effectively creates a decision stump.

## Gradient Boosting

Gradient Boosting provides a more flexible nonlinear benchmark.

The project uses conservative settings for:

* Learning rate
* Tree depth
* Number of estimators

The model is particularly useful for studying overfitting in small datasets.

## VotingRegressor

The ensemble combines:

```text
Ridge
+
Random Forest
+
Decision Tree
+
Gradient Boosting
```

Predictions are averaged to investigate whether combining structurally different models can improve forecast stability.

